--TEST--
`Core\Html::join` escapes nothing, so an escaped fragment is written once and a trusted one is written raw — `rule:core-classes/html-template`, `rule:security/launderer-answers-a-carrier`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Every part is already a carrier, so each one passed whichever rule made it
// markup. Escaping here would be the second application the carrier exists to
// make unreachable: `&amp;` would go out as `&amp;amp;`.
array<Core\Html\Markup> $escaped = [
    Core\Html::escape("a & b"),
    Core\Html::escape("<c>"),
];
echo Core\Html::join($escaped, " and " as Core\Html\Markup), "\n";

// The same five characters in a *trusted* part are the markup the author
// wrote, and they travel untouched — this is where `join` differs from the
// sink, which escapes everything that is not a carrier.
array<Core\Html\Markup> $trusted = [
    "<b>a & b</b>" as Core\Html\Markup,
    "<i>'q' > \"p\"</i>" as Core\Html\Markup,
];
echo Core\Html::join($trusted, " & " as Core\Html\Markup), "\n";

// The separator is a carrier for the same reason and gets the same treatment:
// a separator holding markup is written as markup, not as its own text.
echo Core\Html::join($escaped, "</li><li>" as Core\Html\Markup), "\n";

// And the bytes are the ones the fragments carried, which `toSource` is the
// one way to read back out (`rule:core-classes/html-to-source`).
Core\Html\Markup $joined = Core\Html::join($trusted, "" as Core\Html\Markup);
echo Core\Html::toSource($joined, "asserting the joined bytes are the parts' own"), "\n";
--EXPECT--
a &amp; b and &lt;c&gt;
<b>a & b</b> & <i>'q' > "p"</i>
a &amp; b</li><li>&lt;c&gt;
<b>a & b</b><i>'q' > "p"</i>
